      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i have a physics exam coming up and i'm struggling a bit with the concept of quantum entanglement. can you give me a concise, non-mathematical summary of what it actually is and how it's different from just regular classical correlation? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Quantum entanglement is a phenomenon where two or more particles become connected in such a way that their properties, like spin or momentum, are correlated, regardless of the distance between them. It's like a secret handshake between the particles, where they're \"in sync\" even if they're on opposite sides of the universe. \n\nImagine two coins, one in your hand and one on the other side of the room. If you flip the coin in your hand, the coin on the other side will instantly flip too, even if you can't see it. That's similar to classical correlation, where two events are connected by a cause-and-effect relationship.\n\nBut entanglement is different because it happens instantaneously, no matter how far apart the particles are. It's like the particles are \"talking\" to each other in a way that's faster than the speed of light. This is where things get really weird, and it's where quantum mechanics starts to blur the lines between space and time.\n\nThink of it like a game of \"Goblin's Whispering Walls\" – imagine two goblins, one in a cave and one on the other side of a mountain. They can whisper to each other through the walls, but the message",
      "source": "target",
